## Task lifecycle

- Reviewed installed helper: [absolute path to the installed Vibe Setup `workflow.py`].
- Lifecycle contract: [absolute path to the same package's `task-lifecycle.md`].
- Preferences: `.codex/vibe-workflow.toml`; lifecycle and automatic cleanup default
  ON. Preserve `enabled=false`, the `auto_cleanup=false` master opt-out, and
  independent `delete_remote_branches=false` / `delete_worktrees=false` choices.

For normal tasks, use this helper directly; do not rerun full Vibe onboarding.
Inspect first. Before implementation, create/adopt and verify a linked worktree
with `start`, unless lifecycle is explicitly opted out. New task startup processes all eligible pending cleanup records
in this repository without polling or extra model runs. Reusing a retired task
must go through `start` before any writer resumes; it cancels that task's cleanup
handoff. App-managed removals remain with their native owner. Use the returned command
and worker roots for all writes. If verification fails, stop dependent work and
report the exact manual action; never substitute the primary checkout.
With lifecycle enabled, preview setup changes with `inspect --task-id`, apply only
the reviewed plan, and `resume` the same operation after interruption. Preserve customizations.
With `enabled=false`, skip task-bound setup/publication/cleanup operations; no
task record is created. For onboarding or upgrades, use the reviewed component
installer's check/install/check flow in the checkout permitted by existing project
rules. Preserve the opt-out and use ordinary authorized project delivery.

With lifecycle enabled, plan at most four routine checkpoints, or one per phase.
READY, reopen and blocker transitions do not consume that budget. Use a fresh
READY event within an existing milestone when inputs change; preserve old requests
and require all readiness gates. Stop writers and use `sync` with explicit files
and current evidence. PRs start draft `[BLOCKED]`;
`[BLOCKED]`, `[READY]` and `[SUPERSEDED]` are valid lifecycle title states.
Apply `[SUPERSEDED]` through the helper to record its successor and close the PR.
Update the PR before reporting a blocker in chat; if unavailable, report
“blocked; PR update pending”.
Verify remote commit and metadata before marking synchronization complete. READY
requires checks/review, non-draft status and applicable remote readiness. Restore
BLOCKED before further changes; unresolved withdrawal pauses that PR's work.
Preserve human content, distinguish local completion from delivery, attribute
manual confirmations, and provide exact lookup/push/PR actions when automation
fails. Reconcile the same event before retrying; never create duplicate PRs blindly
or auto-merge. Installed settings do not authorize unrelated publication.

## Working procedure

Security validation uses exactly three gates: implementation-complete/READY/
production candidate, release/deployment (including hotfixes and lifecycle opt-out),
or an explicit user/project requirement. The first two need no separate request.
Read [absolute path to the installed Vibe Setup `security-validation.md`].
At a gate, record actual scope, risk R0/R1/R2 and required checks.
Use applicable Trail of Bits methods proportionally, preserving current
model/provider/reasoning/authentication and permissions. R0 requires an impact
review; R1 adds targeted security validation; R2 adds independent security review.
Keep reports private and bind complete passing evidence to the current snapshot.
Missing, stale, incomplete or failing required evidence blocks readiness/release.
Before release, run the helper's read-only `check-security` against a clean checkout
of the exact release commit, alongside existing release checks. This also applies
when lifecycle is disabled. Preserve blocked checkpoint and withdrawal paths;
manual confirmations cannot bypass security. External release pipelines need an
explicit preflight integration; hooks do not enforce this entire policy.
Within each gate, check official Trail of Bits updates and review applicable
changes before adopting a pinned revision. Record `trailofbits-update-review`
evidence at every tier. Reuse a metadata lookup within the same gate run; refresh
it for a later/resumed run. A newer version alone does not require adoption or
a full audit. Reuse proven applicable evidence and rerun only invalidated checks.
Draft worktrees get normal focused implementation tests, not automatic audits.
Changes/findings invalidate evidence without creating another review trigger;
keep incomplete drafts BLOCKED and resolve required gaps before gate acceptance.

Read active instructions, relevant code and contracts before editing. Preserve
existing work. Define acceptance checks, make the smallest complete change, run
relevant checks and independently review nontrivial changes. State assumptions,
unrun checks and limitations. Distinguish source, fixtures and deployment evidence.
Keep durable facts with source, verification date and invalidation triggers; keep
temporary handoffs in the existing task record. Resume by checking actual state.

## Delegation and models

Read [the delegation policy and execution contract](.codex/delegation-brief.md)
before choosing a route. Preserve the main model, reasoning, provider,
authentication and permissions. Inspect actual native model/effort support and
effective defaults; availability does not grant authorization to switch.
Do not add a provider, proxy or paid API to enable delegation.
When explicitly authorized, adopt `[worker_selection]` in `.codex/vibe-workflow.toml`
with `enabled=true` and the scoped `authorization_ref`; absence/false preserves
inheritance. The Coordinator classifies difficulty and risk, prefers qualified
approved local models with admitted capacity, and otherwise selects an eligible
native model/effort through supported controls. Record requested/observed settings
and selection reasons privately. Preserve Coordinator settings and plugin approval
boundaries; test actual App/VS Code controls separately from task ability/savings.

The main agent is Coordinator. Assign each worker a [role and separate specialty](.codex/delegation-brief.md#role-and-specialty),
carrying them through local execution and native fallback using the installed
contract. These labels never select native profiles, change execution settings or
prove competence. Dashboard badge delivery needs separate evidence.

Use the [task/specialty guide](.codex/delegation-brief.md#task-and-specialty-guide)
to define the assignment's required ability. Match evidence to the concrete model
and task scope; an idle runtime does not prove competence. Mark missing matching
evidence **Not assessed**, keep approval and runtime availability separate, and
follow the linked plugin assessment contract rather than inventing qualifications.

Prefer the installed `local-model-delegation` plugin for suitable bounded tasks
when routing is enabled, the exact model/scope is approved, the complete task fits
assessed capability/context limits, and safe capacity is available. Follow its
[own workflow](https://github.com/fcoouto/local-model-delegation/blob/main/skills/local-delegation/SKILL.md)
using docs matching the installed version for discovery, execution, verification
and fallback. Missing/disabled or ineligible local routes keep work native;
ordinary work does not authorize new approvals or activation. Explicit user
restrictions override this preference.
Local models return proposals, not native subagent reports; native hooks do not
cover their HTTP calls. The coordinator supplies constraints, verifies results
and owns integration. Native fallback obeys both the plugin's dispatch protocol
and the native contract below.
Keep current route/model evidence and qualification gaps in [existing private
setup/quality record]; link this policy from [existing documentation entry point].

For terminal-task cleanup, use one bounded worker on a lightweight compatible native
model, selected from the current host without hard-coded model names. Follow the
cleanup profile in `.codex/delegation-brief.md`: one Python helper invocation, a
compact result, no code review, project test reruns, polling or silent upgrade to
an expensive worker. Preserve coordinator settings, authentication and permissions.
Apply `[SUPERSEDED]` through `supersede` with a verified successor; it closes the
predecessor PR and records replacement ownership. Both cleanup options default ON:
remote branches after merged/closed PRs, worktrees after merge/recorded supersession.
Honor independent opt-outs and the legacy master switch; keep local branch refs.
Verify eligible remote deletion first when enabled. For managed worktrees, follow
`native_action` through the native owner, then run `cleanup` from outside the target
to confirm removal. Queued archival and blocked cleanup remain pending with a
precise action. Preserve dirty/shared/active resources and ambiguous ownership.

Use authorized native subagents when native execution is selected and independent
work, specialization or context isolation justifies coordination. Before spawning,
write the bounded JSON declaration from
[the delegation contract](.codex/delegation-brief.md) to
the active harness root's `.codex-delegations/<task_name>.json`. Name that root and
the software repository separately in the native message; use that exact task name
and one explicit safe harness-root-relative declaration pointer. State command
directories and read/write path bases in the existing context/instructions fields.
Do not reinterpret the pointer using a nearer Git root after changing directories.
Require the child to read it and compare the assigned scope. Compare trusted native
identity when available; otherwise report binding unverified without inventing
identity or blocking solely because host identity is unavailable.
Assign disjoint writable files/resources; serialize dependencies and shared
resources. Reviewers work read-only. Confirm a previous worker has stopped before
taking over its files. The coordinator verifies diffs/evidence and owns integration.

Return exactly one standalone JSON report fence tagged `delegation-report` or
`json`, using the contract's exact schema. Multiple accepted fences are ambiguous;
unfenced JSON is not accepted.

Trusted hooks reject structurally invalid intercepted dispatches and request one
missing-report correction. They do not sandbox workers, verify report truth or
prove ownership. Verify actual invocation on each supported client after changes.

Continue authorized work after status questions and around independent blockers.
Respect pauses, cancellations and finite budgets. Do not claim background work
without an active process. Use the user's publication policy after checks, review
and semantic documentation review; installation grants no deployment authority.
